Targets: wasm
Releases: 9.0.0-
Subject: Make fsync the storage barrier of the browser build
Purpose: PostgreSQL's durability contract is "fsync returned, so the data is stored": it fsyncs the WAL before a commit returns, data files at checkpoints and pg_control after them. In a browser the files live in memory and are stored in IndexedDB, so the browser link (PG_WASM_BROWSER) wraps the library calls fsync() and fdatasync() (wasm-ld --wrap: every reference in the server goes to __wrap_fsync / __wrap_fdatasync, no PostgreSQL source file changes, so the server sources stay untouched). The wrappers call Module.pgBarrier(): the page-side storage writes everything changed since the previous barrier in one IndexedDB transaction with strict durability and resolves when that transaction is complete (-1 when it failed, which becomes EIO, and PostgreSQL treats a failed WAL fsync as PANIC and stops, so a commit that was not stored is never reported). Every barrier therefore stores a consistent generation of all files together, and PostgreSQL's own WAL recovery handles an interruption between barriers. The node build is unchanged (real fsync); without Module.pgBarrier the barrier does nothing (memory-only database).
Components: src/backend/port/wasm_embed.c, src/include/port/wasm_embed.h
Checks: tests/browser: a committed transaction survives killing the Worker, a failing store (fault injection) stops the engine without reporting the commit, an interrupted barrier leaves the previous generation
Origin: own

diff --git a/src/backend/port/wasm_embed.c b/src/backend/port/wasm_embed.c
--- a/src/backend/port/wasm_embed.c
+++ b/src/backend/port/wasm_embed.c
@@ -46,6 +46,40 @@
 	return Module.pgWrite(src, len);
 });
 
+/*
+ * The storage barrier.  PostgreSQL calls fsync() or fdatasync() on the WAL before a commit returns and on data files
+ * and pg_control at checkpoints.  The browser link wraps both (-Wl,--wrap=fsync -Wl,--wrap=fdatasync), so those calls
+ * arrive here.  Module.pgBarrier() writes everything changed since the last barrier to IndexedDB in one transaction
+ * and resolves when that transaction is complete: 0, or -1 when it failed, which makes the call fail with EIO and
+ * stops PostgreSQL, so a commit that was not stored is never reported.  With no Module.pgBarrier (memory-only
+ * database) it does nothing.
+ */
+EM_ASYNC_JS(int, wasm_persist_barrier, (void),
+{
+	if (!Module.pgBarrier)
+		return 0;
+	return await Module.pgBarrier();
+});
+
+extern int	__wrap_fsync(int fd);
+extern int	__wrap_fdatasync(int fd);
+
+int
+__wrap_fsync(int fd)
+{
+	int			rc = wasm_persist_barrier();
+
+	if (rc != 0)
+		errno = EIO;
+	return rc;
+}
+
+int
+__wrap_fdatasync(int fd)
+{
+	return __wrap_fsync(fd);
+}
+
 #else							/* !PG_WASM_BROWSER */
 
 int
diff --git a/src/include/port/wasm_embed.h b/src/include/port/wasm_embed.h
--- a/src/include/port/wasm_embed.h
+++ b/src/include/port/wasm_embed.h
@@ -22,4 +22,9 @@
 /* set up the protocol buffers and the "remote" output destination (pqcomm.c) */
 extern void pq_init_embedded(void);
 
+#ifdef PG_WASM_BROWSER
+/* wait until the database is stored (see wasm_embed.c); 0, or -1 when storing failed */
+extern int	wasm_persist_barrier(void);
+#endif
+
 #endif							/* WASM_EMBED_H */
